WEBVTT

00:01.000 --> 00:04.830
Your model's likelihood takes seconds, or minutes, to compute.

00:05.330 --> 00:08.280
You want the posterior over its parameters.

00:08.630 --> 00:12.920
The posterior tells you how plausible each setting is, given your data.

00:13.620 --> 00:15.800
Picture the posterior as a landscape.

00:16.150 --> 00:19.190
The higher the ground, the more plausible the setting.

00:20.290 --> 00:23.680
The textbook method is Markov chain Monte Carlo.

00:24.030 --> 00:27.020
It needs tens of thousands of evaluations.

00:27.520 --> 00:30.340
At a few minutes each, you would wait weeks.

00:31.440 --> 00:36.570
Bayesian optimization finds the highest point of a landscape in far fewer evaluations.

00:36.920 --> 00:42.030
It learns the landscape from the points it has seen, then decides where to explore next.

00:42.630 --> 00:46.260
PyVBMC does the same for the whole posterior.

00:47.360 --> 00:48.740
We start in the dark.

00:49.240 --> 00:53.020
Each evaluation reveals the height at a single point.

00:54.120 --> 00:57.930
From these few points, PyVBMC builds a surrogate.

00:58.280 --> 01:02.180
The surrogate is a cheap statistical model of the whole landscape.

01:02.680 --> 01:05.340
At the evaluated points it holds still.

01:05.690 --> 01:07.770
Everywhere else it wobbles.

01:08.270 --> 01:10.540
The more it wobbles, the less it knows.

01:11.640 --> 01:15.580
On the surrogate, PyVBMC fits a mixture of Gaussians.

01:16.180 --> 01:18.340
Why not use the surrogate itself?

01:18.690 --> 01:22.810
It gives a height at every point, but it's not directly usable.

01:23.260 --> 01:27.520
A mixture of Gaussians you can sample and compute with, exactly.*

01:28.120 --> 01:31.000
This is its first guess at the posterior.

01:31.350 --> 01:33.360
For now it is a crude one.

01:34.460 --> 01:36.360
Where to evaluate next?

01:36.860 --> 01:39.540
An optimizer would head for the peak.

01:39.890 --> 01:45.940
PyVBMC is after the whole posterior, so it explores places that are plausible and still uncertain.

01:49.460 --> 01:50.510
Evaluate.

01:51.110 --> 01:52.600
Update the surrogate.

01:53.200 --> 01:54.470
Refit the mixture.

01:57.070 --> 02:00.150
Round after round, the wobbling dies down.

02:01.650 --> 02:04.840
The mixture grows new components to follow the ridge.

02:13.570 --> 02:16.460
PyVBMC never saw the true landscape.

02:17.060 --> 02:18.100
Here it is.

02:19.200 --> 02:22.730
The same calculation gives an estimate of the model's evidence.

02:23.080 --> 02:25.600
You need that number to compare two models.

02:26.100 --> 02:29.070
The estimate lands close to the true value.

02:30.470 --> 02:33.390
All this from a hundred evaluations.

02:33.890 --> 02:37.680
Markov chain Monte Carlo would have needed tens of thousands.

02:38.380 --> 02:40.880
Weeks of waiting became an afternoon.

02:41.480 --> 02:45.170
At a second per evaluation, hours become minutes.
